bin/
 - contains some standalone programs

bin/fyvt-unterm.c
 - an example program using the library to reconstruct the final state of a
   terminal after replaying captured input

bin/fyvt-ctrl.c
 - a helper program that emits sequences to control a running terminal

bin/fyvt-dump.c
 - an example program using the parser layer to interpret captured input

CODE-MAP
 - high-level list and description of files in the repository

CONTRIBUTING
 - documentation explaining how developers can contribute fixes and features

doc/
 - contains documentation

doc/seqs.txt
 - documents the sequences recognised by the library

include/libfyvterm.h
 - umbrella include file; pulls in everything under include/libfyvterm/

include/libfyvterm/
 - the public headers, split by layer: -util.h (shared types, the
   FYVT_EXPORT macro), -core.h (the terminal instance), -keys.h (keyboard
   and mouse), -parser.h, -state.h, -screen.h

LICENSE
 - legalese

CMakeLists.txt
 - main build file; src/, bin/ and t/ have their own

cmake/
 - package config and pkg-config templates, and the JoinPaths helper

scripts/
 - generators for the checked-in Unicode and encoding tables

src/
 - contains the source code for the library

src/encoding.c
 - handles mapping ISO/IEC 2022 alternate character sets into Unicode
   codepoints

src/keyboard.c
 - handles sending reported keyboard events to the output stream

src/mouse.c
 - handles sending reported mouse events to the output stream

src/parser.c
 - parses bytes from the input stream into parser-level events

src/pen.c
 - interprets SGR sequences and maintains current rendering attributes

src/screen.c
 - uses state-level events to maintain a buffer of current screen contents

src/state.c
 - follows parser-level events to keep track of the overall terminal state

src/unicode.c
 - utility functions for Unicode and UTF-8 handling

src/fyvt.c
 - toplevel object state and miscellaneous functions

src/fyvt_internal.h
 - include file for definitions private to the library's internals

t/
 - contains unit tests

t/harness.c
 - the command interpreter the test scripts drive; turns a line of the test
   command language into library calls and records the callback events

t/run-test.c
 - reads a .test script, drives the harness with it, and compares the
   recorded events against the script's expectations. Links the harness
   directly, so a test run is a single process with no interpreter
